Records JSON:
[{"record_id":"rec_000081","title":"UniTCM: A computational reverse pharmacology framework for decoding formula–disease interactions in Traditional Chinese Medicine","abstract":"UniTCM UniTCM: A computational reverse pharmacology framework for decoding formula–disease interactions in Traditional Chinese Medicine UniTCM is a comprehensive web-based platform that transforms Traditional Chinese Medicine (TCM) knowledge into actionable molecular insights. It integrates five synergistic layers — standardization, clinical discovery, molecular analysis, systems verification, and analytical tools — into a unified closed-loop framework. The live website is hosted at: https://unitcm.qfxulab.com/ Table of Contents Overview System Architecture Module 1: Standardization Layer Module 2: Clinical Discovery Layer Module 3: Molecular Analysis Layer Module 4: Systems Verification Layer Module 5: Analytical Tools Technology Stack Getting Started API Documentation Citation License Overview Traditional Chinese Medicine (TCM) represents one of the oldest and most comprehensive medical systems in the world, yet its modernization faces three fundamental barriers: Terminology heterogeneity — TCM concepts lack standardized mapping to modern biomedical terminology Mechanistic opacity — The molecular mechanisms underlying TCM formulas remain largely unknown Absence of systematic safety evaluation — TCM ingredients lack comprehensive ADMET profiling UniTCM addresses these challenges through a \"from bedside to bench\" reverse pharmacology paradigm, organized into five synergistic layers. Key Statistics Category Count Ontology Entities 632 Bilingual Terms 52,275 Mechanism Records 15,195 Formula Records >250,000 ICD-11 Diseases 3,204 Herb Entries 28,728 Active Ingredients 87,040 ADMET Endpoints 31 Multi-omics Datasets 1,881 Pre-computed Studies 1,037 System Architecture ┌───────────────────────────────┐ │ Layer 5: Analytical Tools │ │ (Predict Target-SEA · Target2NP · MIDAS · NetVis · API) │ ├───────────────────────────────┤ │ Layer 4: Systems Verification │ │ (TCMomics Repository · TCM Transcriptome Hub) │ ├───────────────────────────────┤ │ Layer 3: Molecular Analysis │ │ (Herb Explorer · Ingredient Explorer · Target Prediction) │├───────────────────────────────┤ │ Layer 2: Clinical Discovery │ │ (Disease–Formula Atlas >250,000 formulas) │ ├───────────────────────────────┤ │ Layer 1: Standardization │ │ (Ontology · Bilingual Corpus · Molecular Mechanisms) │ └───────────────────────────────┘ Each layer builds upon the outputs of the preceding one, creating a complete pipeline from raw TKM knowledge to computational analysis. Module 1: Standardization Layer The Standardization Module addresses the most fundamental barrier to TCM modernization: the absence of a unified conceptual and linguistic framework. TCM Ontology System A four-level hierarchical ontology encompassing 14 top-level categories, 487 subcategories, and 632 total entities with 29 external mappings to established biomedical ontologies (UMLS, ICD-11, GO, MeSH, NCBI Taxonomy). Top-level categories: Herb, Formula, Disease, Syndrome, Symptom, Acupoint, Meridian, Constitution, Therapeutic Method, Processing Method, Dosage Form, Nature and Flavor, Toxicity, Bioactive Ingredient Bilingual TCM Corpus The most comprehensive bilingual (Chinese–English) TCM terminology corpus compiled to date, integrating 13 authoritative sources across three authority tiers: Tier 1 — International Standards: WHO International Standard Terminologies, WFCMS International Standard Tier 2 — National Standards & Dictionaries: Chinese Pharmacopoeia, Nigel Wiseman's dictionary, and more Tier 3 — Specialized Databases: TCMSP, ETCM, SymMap, HERB, CMAPH, and specialist glossaries After systematic deduplication, the final corpus contains 52,275 unique standardized terms. Molecular Mechanisms Lexicon Bridges classical TCM terminology to modern biomedical concepts, constructed from 12,090 peer-reviewed articles (PubMed and CNKI), yielding 15,195 structured records. Module 2: Clinical Discovery Layer Disease–Formula Atlas The largest curated repository of TCM formula–disease associations assembled to date, covering >250,000 formula records across 30 structured fields from over 22 authoritative sources. Major data sources include the 10-volume Zhongyifangji Dacidian (~96,000 formulas), specialized collections covering oncology, gynecology, nephrology, dermatology, cardiovascular, and neurology, plus digital databases ETCM and HERB. Disease Standardization All classical indications are mapped to ICD-11-standardized disease codes via a custom semantic similarity algorithm, enabling structured queries across 3,204 diseases. Historical Dosage Normalization Ancient TCM formulas span multiple dynasties, each with distinct weight-and-measure systems. UniTCM implements a three-stage LLM-assisted pipeline that converts all raw dosage expressions into modern grams, supporting quantitative analyses across two millennia of clinical practice. Dynasty Group Period 1 两 → g Notes Qin / Han (秦汉) 221 BCE – 220 CE 15.6 g Earliest unified weight standard Sui / Tang (隋唐) 581 – 907 41.3 g Large-scale reform Song / Jin / Yuan (宋金元) 960 – 1368 40.0 g Stable across three dynasties Ming / Qing (明清) 1368 – 1912 37.3 g Kuping standard Modern clinical ref (现代折算) Contemporary 15.0 g Simplified safety convention Module 3: Molecular Analysis Layer Herb Explorer Contains 28,728 unique herb entries — substantially exceeding the coverage of all existing platforms (HERB: 7,263; TCMSP: 499). Each entry is annotated with taxonomic classification, medicinal properties, traditional indications, and source provenance. Ingredient Explorer & ADMET Profiling Contains 87,040 unique active ingredients with comprehensive ADMET predictions covering 31 endpoints across five domains: Absorption: Caco-2 permeability, human intestinal absorption, P-gp substrate/inhibitor Distribution: BBB penetration, plasma protein binding, volume of distribution Metabolism: CYP450 substrate/inhibitor profiles (CYP1A2, CYP2C9, CYP2C19, CYP2D6, CYP3A4) Excretion: Half-life, clearance rate Toxicity: hERG inhibition, AMES mutagenicity, hepatotoxicity, skin sensitization, carcinogenicity Ingredient–Target Prediction Dual-strategy target prediction pipeline combining: SEA — Structural Similarity: Similarity Ensemble Approach based on ChEMBL v35 (~2 million compounds, ~5,000 human protein targets) DrugCLIP — Deep Learning: Contrastive learning framework that jointly embeds molecular structures and protein binding pockets Module 4: Systems Verification Layer TCMomics Repository A dedicated multi-omics repository curating 1,881 datasets assembled via systematic search of GEO, ArrayExpress, PRIDE, MetaboLights, iProX, and SRA using 1,200+ TCM-related keywords, followed by LLM-based relevance classification (94.2% accuracy) and manual curation. TCM Transcriptome Hub Pre-computed bioinformatics analyses for 1,037 transcriptomic studies spanning 54 organisms and 451 TCM entities. Each study is processed through a standardized pipeline: Normalization & QC Differential Expression Pathway Enrichment (GO / KEGG) GSEA PPI Network & Hub Genes Immune Infiltration Transcription Factor Prediction Module 5: Analytical Tools Predict Target-SEA An interactive online tool that predicts potential protein targets for user-supplied small molecules via the Similarity Ensemble Approach (SEA), backed by ChEMBL v35 (~2 million active compounds, ~5,000 human protein targets). Target2NP A systematically curated resource that aggregates natural product–target interaction data from 5 major databases, providing an evidence-graded view of over 912,000 interaction records. MIDAS (Multi-source Integrated Disease Association Search) Aggregates gene–disease association evidence from 11 authoritative biomedical databases, encompassing over 5.75 million association records. Supports six integrated analytical modes. NetVis (Network Pharmacology Visualization Workbench) A full-featured, browser-based interactive workbench for exploring the five-layer TCM pharmacological network: Disease ↔ Formula ↔ Herb ↔ Compound ↔ Target, rendered via WebGL-accelerated Sigma.js v3. API & Programmatic Access Comprehensive RESTful API with companion packages for R and Python, enabling programmatic access to all platform data and analytical services. Technology Stack Backend Framework: Django 5.x with Django Ninja (REST API) Authentication: JWT (via ninja-jwt) Database: PostgreSQL Cache / Queue: Redis (caching, Celery broker) Object Storage: Alibaba Cloud OSS (via django-oss-storage) Task Scheduler: Django-crontab Frontend Framework: Vue 3 (Composition API) UI Library: Element Plus Build Tool: Vite Charts: ECharts Network Visualization: Sigma.js v3 Computational Tools Molecular Fingerprinting: RDKit (ECFP4) Target Prediction: SEA algorithm, DrugCLIP deep learning Statistical Analysis: scipy, statsmodels Bioinformatics: limma, DESeq2, clusterProfiler, CIBERSORT Getting Started Prerequisites Python 3.10+ Node.js 18+ PostgreSQL 15+ Redis Backend Setup # Clone the repository git clone cd uni-tcm/backend # Create virtual environment python -m venv venv source venv/bin/activate # On Windows: venv\\Scripts\\activate # Install dependencies pip install -r requirements.txt # Copy environment file and adjust settings cp .env.example .env # Edit .env with your local configuration # Run database migrations python manage.py migrate # Start development server python manage.py runserver Frontend Setup cd uni-tcm/frontend # Install dependencies npm install # Start development server npm run dev Docker Deployment docker-compose up -d","doi":"10.5281/zenodo.21711675","year":"2026","venue":"Zenodo (CERN European Organization for Nuclear Research)","sources":["openalex"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000081","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"A web-based platform and curated resource for TCM formula-disease, ontology, corpus, and molecular mechanism data, not a primary model paper. It explicitly includes bilingual terminology and LLM-assisted normalization/analysis.","evidence_snippet":"\"web-based platform\"; \"Bilingual TCM Corpus\"; \"LLM-assisted pipeline\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000081","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a web platform and reverse-pharmacology resource, not a new generative text-bio model. The abstract emphasizes curation, prediction tools, and APIs rather than a generative architecture.","evidence_snippet":"\"comprehensive web-based platform\"; \"Predict Target-SEA · Target2NP · MIDAS · NetVis · API\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"benchmark_resource","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000082","title":"Unify learns cellular evolution with universal multimodal embeddings","abstract":"Integrating single-cell RNA-sequencing (scRNA-seq) data across species is still technically challenging. Here, the authors report a transfer learning framework designed to integrate scRNA-seq data across species by combining RNA expression with embeddings from protein language models and general-purpose language models. Integrating single-cell RNA-sequencing (scRNA-seq) data across species is hindered by evolutionary divergence, technical batch effects, and the reliance on one-to-one orthologs. Here, we present Unify, a transfer learning methodology that learns universal cell embeddings by defining functionally coherent, multi-modal macrogenes. This is achieved by combining RNA expression with embeddings from protein language models and general-purpose language models. Unify transcends species boundaries, enabling cross-species comparisons beyond strict gene-level homology. Unify corrects batch effects while preserving conserved biological signals across vast evolutionary distances and enables more accurate prediction of perturbation responses across species, such as from mouse to human. Applied to species separated by over 700 million years, Unify reconstructs more accurate multi-species cell-type evolutionary trees and uncovers convergent gene programs. Together, these results establish Unify as a powerful method for comparative single-cell genomics and evolutionary biology.","doi":"10.1038/s41467-026-76230-y","year":"2026","venue":"Nature Communications","sources":["openalex","semantic_scholar","springernature"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000082","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This presents Unify as a transfer learning framework that learns universal cell embeddings. It combines scRNA-seq with protein language model and general-purpose language model embeddings.","evidence_snippet":"\"combining RNA expression with embeddings from protein language models and general-purpose language models\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000082","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"none","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"Unify is presented as a transfer-learning framework for universal cell embeddings, but the abstract does not describe a generative architecture. It does provide foundation-model-like evidence through use of protein language models and general-purpose language models for cross-species transfer.","evidence_snippet":"\"transfer learning framework\"; \"combining RNA expression with embeddings from protein language models and general-purpose language models\"; \"learns universal cell embeddings\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"none","adjudication_reason":"fallback_unresolved","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"yes"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000084","title":"A generative AI multi-agent framework with integrated XAI governance for cancer diagnostics: from multi-omics interpretation to lifestyle risk stratification","abstract":"Background Cancer diagnostics is being reshaped by rapid advances in artificial intelligence, yet a persistent gap separates computational performance from clinical trust. Systematic reviews confirm that 83% of XAI studies in oncology excluded clinicians from development or evaluation, 87% lacked rigorous assessment of XAI explanations, and no universally accepted quality metrics for XAI outputs currently exist. Concurrently, generative AI (GenAI) is entering oncology at an unprecedented pace, yet it operates largely without formal interpretability governance. Aim This review aimed to (i) synthesize the documented gaps in XAI applications for cancer diagnostics from peer-reviewed literature, (ii) evaluate the state and limitations of GenAI in oncological settings, and (iii) propose a conceptual framework of three GenAI-powered, XAI-governed agents designed to address these gaps within a systems biology context. Review Findings A narrative synthesis of peer-reviewed literature published between 2020 and 2026 across PubMed, Scopus, and Web of Science identified four critical, recurrent gaps: systematic exclusion of clinicians from XAI development, the absence of standardized evaluation metrics, incomplete cross-omics explanations, and the near absence of lifestyle-driven XAI models for cancer risk. GenAI is accelerating in oncology but introduces additional safety concerns, including hallucinations, non-deterministic outputs, and the illusion of transparency through chain-of-thought reasoning. Framework Proposal Three specialized agents are proposed: a Multi-Omics XAI Agent (MO-XAI Agent) for cross-layer biological data interpretation, a Clinician Trust and Communication Agent (CTC Agent) for structured explanation translation and quality scoring, and a Lifestyle-Driven Cancer Risk Stratification Agent (LRS Agent) for modifiable risk factor analysis. Each agent uses a large language model as the computational backbone with SHAP, LIME, or graph-level XAI methods as governance layers. Conclusion To our knowledge, this framework represents the first conceptual architecture to integrate agentic GenAI with systematic XAI governance for cancer diagnostics, offering a clinically grounded, biologically interpretable, and regulatorily aligned design specification for future implementation research.","doi":"10.3389/fsysb.2026.1892418","year":"2026","venue":"Frontiers in Systems Biology","sources":["openalex","semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000084","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a review with a conceptual framework proposal, not an implemented candidate model paper. It discusses LLM-based agents for cancer diagnostics and multi-omics interpretation.","evidence_snippet":"\"This review aimed to synthesize\"; \"propose a conceptual framework\"; \"large language model as the computational backbone\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000084","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"foundation_model_evidence_unclear","decision_rationale":"The abstract is a review with a conceptual framework, but it explicitly proposes GenAI-powered agents using an LLM backbone. That is generative in description, but the foundation-model evidence is only conceptual rather than a concrete candidate model.","evidence_snippet":"\"This review aimed to... evaluate the state and limitations of GenAI\"; \"Each agent uses a large language model as the computational backbone\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","generative_model_present":"yes","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000090","title":"Generative AI-augmented transcriptomic and microbiome analysis across inflammatory and fibrotic disease states in Crohn’s disease","abstract":"Intestinal fibrosis is a major complication of Crohn’s disease (CD), a subtype of inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) driven by chronic inflammation and resulting in irreversible structural damage requiring surgery. However, the molecular differences between inflammatory and fibrotic CD remain poorly defined.\n \n \n \n \n Here, we developed an integrated multi-omics framework combining transcriptomics, microbiome analysis, and generative AI to characterise transcriptomic differences across non-IBD (\n n\n  = 176), baseline CD (\n n\n  = 187), and fibrosis CD (\n n\n  = 85) tissues. Bulk and single-cell RNA-seq and 16S rRNA datasets were integrated, and machine learning identified disease-stage associated features.\n \n \n \n \n \n A shared set of 43 genes between baseline and fibrotic CD was organised into three modules: Module 1 (S100A8, TREM1, CXCL1) linked to innate immune activation which was upregulated in fibrosis CD; Module 2 (FABP6, MGAM, ALDOB) reflecting epithelial metabolic dysfunction which was upregulated in baseline CD; and Module 3 (CHI3L1, SAA2-SAA4, IL1RN) associated with epithelial stress and loss of barrier integrity. GSVA highlighted LCN2 and MMP3 across disease states. Microbiome analysis showed depletion of SCFA-producing genera (\n Faecalibacterium\n ,\n Anaerostipes\n ,\n Coprococcus\n ,\n Ruminococcus\n ) and enrichment of\n Bilophila\n and\n Bacteroides\n . Notably, LLM-guided augmentation improved model stability and facilitated the identification of key fibrosis-associated genes, including IL-23R, TNF-α, and TGF-β.\n \n \n \n \n These findings suggest that intestinal fibrosis in CD does not represent a separate molecular state, but a reconfigured inflammatory condition characterised by persistent immune activation, epithelial dysfunction, and altered host-microbiome interactions.","doi":"10.3389/frai.2026.1881820","year":"2026","venue":"Frontiers in Artificial Intelligence","sources":["openalex","semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000090","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"unclear","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"text_component_unclear","decision_rationale":"This is an omics analysis framework with generative AI used to augment and stabilize the model, not a clearly described text-bio foundational model. The abstract does not specify a substantive language model component.","evidence_snippet":"\"integrated multi-omics framework combining transcriptomics, microbiome analysis, and generative AI\" and \"LLM-guided augmentation improved model stability\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000090","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"The work is presented as a multi-omics analysis framework augmented by generative AI/LLM guidance, but no concrete generative model architecture is described. It reads as an analysis wrapper rather than a candidate foundation model paper.","evidence_snippet":"\"integrated multi-omics framework combining transcriptomics, microbiome analysis, and generative AI\"; \"LLM-guided augmentation improved model stability\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"application_wrapper","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"unclear","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000091","title":"Artificial Intelligence in Obstetrics: Current Trends and Future Directions","abstract":"Background: Artificial intelligence (AI), which spans machine learning (ML), deep learning (DL), computer vision, and natural language processing (NLP), is now used across obstetric care, including ultrasound interpretation (biometry, anomaly detection), fetal monitoring (cardiotocography), maternal risk stratification (preeclampsia, preterm birth, hemorrhage), labor and delivery decision support, genomic screening, and telehealth. Methods: We conducted a narrative (non-systematic) review of the literature published between 2016 and 2026, distinguishing the level of evidence supporting each application. Results: Reported performance is frequently high for image-based tasks such as fetal biometry and anomaly detection (accuracy and AUC often exceeding 0.85), whereas intrapartum CTG analysis remains modest (AUROC ~0.60–0.70, overlapping the inter-observer variability of clinicians). Most published evidence is retrospective and internally validated; comparatively few tools have undergone external or prospective validation, and only a small number have received regulatory clearance. Limitations: The evidence base is heterogeneous, external validation and calibration are often absent, and we did not perform a formal risk-of-bias appraisal. Conclusions: AI has real potential to improve prenatal diagnosis and individualized care, but claims that it is ready for the clinic are often premature. Prospective and external validation, calibration and clinical-utility assessment, transparent reporting, attention to bias and equity, and sustained clinician oversight are prerequisites for safe adoption.","doi":"10.3390/jcm15156059","year":"2026","venue":"Journal of Clinical Medicine","sources":["openalex","semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000091","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"text_bio_bridge_unclear","decision_rationale":"This is explicitly a narrative review of AI applications in obstetrics, not a primary candidate model paper. It mentions NLP among reviewed applications, but not a single substantive text-bio model.","evidence_snippet":"\"We conducted a narrative (non-systematic) review\" and AI spans \"computer vision, and natural language processing (NLP)\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000091","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is explicitly a narrative review of AI applications in obstetrics, not an original model paper. No generative model or foundation-model evidence is presented.","evidence_snippet":"\"We conducted a narrative (non-systematic) review\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000092","title":"Asymmetric dual pathway fusion for histology driven spatial transcriptomic prediction","abstract":"Spatial transcriptomics (ST) measures gene expression at spatial resolution across a tissue section, but its routine clinical use is constrained by high cost, low throughput and tissue consumption. Predicting spatially resolved gene expression directly from the haematoxylin and eosin (H&E) slides already produced by routine pathology offers a scalable alternative. Existing histology-to-ST predictors, however, rely on a single visual encoder that struggles to resolve fine-grained cellular morphology and broader tissue architecture simultaneously—two complementary scales that jointly shape transcriptional state. We introduce AsymST, a dual-pathway framework in which a lightweight convolutional encoder and a pathology foundation model are coupled by an asymmetric cross-attention block: the foundation-model tokens selectively absorb local texture cues from the convolutional branch, but the convolutional tokens are not updated in return, so neither branch homogenises the other. A deep latent information bottleneck provides the main regression pathway, while shallow per-branch auxiliary heads provide explicit branch-level supervision. Across 10 cancer cohorts and 9 spot- and slide-based baselines, AsymST attains the highest mean per-gene Pearson correlation (0.429), ranks first on 9 of 10 cohorts, and remains significantly better than each of the four strongest baselines in a cohort-level Wilcoxon test that treats cancer cohort as the independent unit (BH-adjusted $$q_{\\textrm{BH}}\\!=\\!0.002$$ for all four comparisons). Despite using no between-spot spatial modelling at training time, AsymST also achieves higher neighbourhood-scale spatial coherence than slide-based methods at every scale tested, suggesting that spatial coherence can emerge from a sufficiently expressive per-spot visual representation. The accuracy gains concentrate on biologically interpretable immune, inflammatory and stromal programs of the tumour microenvironment, and translate into recovery of B-cell-rich regions resembling tertiary lymphoid structures on a representative lung adenocarcinoma slide—a proof of concept for inferring clinically informative tumour-immunology readouts directly from routine histology.","doi":"10.1038/s41598-026-63426-x","year":"2026","venue":"Scientific Reports","sources":["openalex"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000092","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"AsymST is a primary model for predicting spatial transcriptomics from histology images, but the abstract describes image and gene-expression modeling rather than a text/language component.","evidence_snippet":"\"predicting spatially resolved gene expression directly from the haematoxylin and eosin (H&E) slides\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000092","paper_type":"application_wrapper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"AsymST is a predictive histology-to-spatial-transcriptomics framework that uses a pathology foundation model as one component. The abstract does not describe a genuinely generative model or a new foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"\"dual-pathway framework\"; \"a pathology foundation model are coupled\"; \"predicting spatially resolved gene expression\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_text_component","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}}]

